## Answer: Reddit Ads had the biggest CPA change

| Platform | CPA prior wk | CPA last wk | Δ CPA | Spend Δ | Conversions Δ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Reddit Ads** | $125.47 | $110.75 | **−$14.72 (−11.7%)** | +$22.41 (+2.98%) | 6 → 7 |
| Meta Ads | $197.99 | $190.00 | −$7.99 (−4.0%) | −$721.85 (−19.2%) | 19 → 16 |
| Google Ads | $192.05 | $191.18 | −$0.87 (−0.45%, flat) | +$2,651.92 (+49.3%) | 28 → 42 |

## What likely drove each

- **Reddit (−11.7%)** — a conversion-volume story, not efficiency: spend was nearly flat (+$22.41) and conversions ticked 6 → 7. One extra conversion at ~$775/week spend is enough to produce the entire CPA move. Treat this as **within noise**, not a confirmed efficiency gain.
- **Meta (−4.0%)** — spend fell faster (−19.2%) than conversions (−15.8%), so CPA drifted down with the budget pullback. The same-direction move fits a pacing/budget reduction; a tracking or delivery problem would typically show conversions falling while spend held.
- **Google (flat)** — the notable story is scale, not CPA: spend +49.3% bought a proportional +50.0% conversion increase, so incremental dollars converted at the prior week's ~$191–192 CPA. No efficiency deterioration to explain.

## Caveats for the report

- **Small volumes:** 6–7 conversions/week (Reddit), 16–19 (Meta), 28–42 (Google). All % changes here sit on small bases; single conversions swing Reddit CPA materially.
- **Attribution is account-level only.** None of the three platforms' available reporting exposes campaign-level rows, so drivers above are inferred from totals, not named campaigns. Campaign-level breakdowns (e.g., in Ads Manager) would be the follow-up if any of these moves need explaining further.
- No metric conflicts to flag across sources — all figures are platform-reported media metrics; no warehouse/pipeline figures were mixed in.

One implication worth carrying into recommendations: Google's spend scale-up came at flat CPA, so it should stay in place — no basis in this data for pulling it back. Happy to draft the full weekly report (per-platform sections + one recommendation each) from these same numbers if you want it.
